                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input is a grid with a gray (5) vertical line down the center. The left and right parts are 3x3. For each square colored on both left and right, output red (2) at that position.\n\nActually the verifier: it computes whether the dividing line column (gray line) is uniform color. Let me trace.\n\nx0 = width(I) = 7. x1 = 3. x2 = tojvec(3) = (0,3). x3 = height = 3. x4 = 2. x5 = astuple(2,3) = (2,3). x6 = connect((0,3),(2,3)) = vertical line column 3. x7 = toobject(x6, I) = colors at column 3 = {5}. x8 = numcolors = 1. x9 = True. So x10 = lefthalf, x11 = righthalf. x12 = lefthalf(I) - 3x3 left part. x13 = righthalf(I) - 3x3 right part. x14 = palette(x12) = {0,1}. x15 = other = 1 (nonzero color... hmm \"other\" removes 1? No, removes ZERO=0, so other gives 1). x17 = 1. x20 = indices of color 1 in left. x21 = indices of color 1 in right. x22 = intersection. x23 = fill zeros canvas with 2 at intersection.\n\nSo output = positions where both left and right have color 1 (in example, color 1).\n\nLet me verify with example: left = \n[1,0,0]\n[0,0,1]\n[1,1,1]\nright = \n[1,0,1]\n[1,1,1]\n[1,1,1]\nIntersection of 1-positions: \n(0,0): both 1 -> yes 2\n(1,2): both 1 -> yes\n(2,0),(2,1),(2,2): all yes.\nOutput: [2,0,0],[0,0,2],[2,2,2]. Matches.\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. I need to provide input grids that verifier can evaluate. The rule: input grid width odd, gray line center. The nonzero color in left/right is 1 (the \"other\" color). Actually note: x15 = other(palette(x12), ZERO) -> removes 0, returns the remaining color (assumes only one nonzero color). So left part has colors {0, c}. If left has only {0,c} that's fine. Intersection uses ofcolor(left, c) & ofcolor(right, c') where c=left nonzero, c'=right nonzero. If they differ, then colors differ? But intersection is over indices, not colors \u2014 so as long as positions overlap, filled red regardless of colors? Wait, ofcolor(x12, x15) is positions in left with color x15; ofcolor(x13, x17) positions in right with color x17. Intersection of positions. So if left has color 1 at pos p and right has color 3 at pos p, intersection includes p, filled 2.\n\nBut palette(x12): if left has {0,1} and right has {0,3}, x15=1, x17=3, works.\n\nBut careful: if left has multiple nonzero colors, \"other\" would fail (remove returns container with still multiple, first gives arbitrary). Let's keep single nonzero color per half, same color ideally, to be safe.\n\nAlso the branch: if numcolors of gray line == 1 then left/right split; else top/bottom split. We want vertical gray line so it uses left/right halves.\n\nLet me design 3 inputs: 3x7 grids with column 3 = 5.\n\nPair 1: distinct pattern.\n\nLet me make simple ones.\n\nInput A:\nleft =\n[1,1,0]\n[0,1,0]\n[0,0,1]\nright =\n[1,0,0]\n[1,1,0]\n[0,0,1]\nIntersection of 1s: left positions {(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(2,2)}; right {(0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(2,2)}. Intersection {(0,0),(1,1),(2,2)} -> diagonal 2s.\nFull row: [1,1,0,5,1,0,0], [0,1,0,5,1,1,0], [0,0,1,5,0,0,1].\n\nPair 2:\nleft:\n[0,1,0]\n[1,1,1]\n[0,1,0]\nright:\n[0,0,0]\n[1,1,1]\n[0,0,0]\nIntersection: left 1s include middle column and center; right 1s middle row. Intersection = {(1,0),(1,1),(1,2)} -> middle row all 2.\nRow: [0,1,0,5,0,0,0],[1,1,1,5,1,1,1],[0,1,0,5,0,0,0].\n\nPair 3: use a different non-gray color, say 3, to show color-agnostic? The rule says \"colored\" so maybe use different colors. But safer to use 1? Actually the verifier: palette left, remove 0 -> other color. If I use left color 3 and right color 4, x15=3, x17=4, intersection positions. Works. But might confuse student; the natural language says \"colored on both\". Let me maybe use color 1 consistently, but one with different color to show it's about being colored (nonzero). Hmm, the student must infer. The rule essentially: overlap of non-zero cells. Using same color 1 in all three is fine but might make student think only color 1 matters. Using different color